centuries have passed but our order has  remained steadfast in its sacred mission  to preserve the balance between man and  half-breed the order 1886 is a big tease  it starts with a cool Victorian setting  introduces werewolves and great guns and  then proceeds to take away the good  skunk the order is cruel in the way it  plays with your expectations  taking a promising idea and then sucking  out all the energy with boring cutscenes  a rage inducing ending and bland  stretches during which nothing is going  on  boring is the best word to describe the  order in general it is as you probably  guessed 1886 and you are Grayson  otherwise known as Galahad one of the  knights of the round table it is a time  of trouble common citizens have begun to  rebel against the gentry possibly a  lying with a race of werewolves the game  alternately refers to as lichens and  half breeds that such a world could be  so lifeless is unfathomable yet the  order pulls it off turning its great  ideas into the dullest game possible you  face lichens early in the game leaping  out of the way and shooting them down  before killing them off for good by  plunging a knife into them and then the  game casts them aside until they're  barely a memory the order pulls them out  of hiding a few more time is mostly for  snooze worthy QuickTime events instead  of you know cool battles as for the  nature of the Lycans where they come  from and all that developer ready at  dawn throws all of that away to focus on  the stale heroes who sit and argue at  the round table every so often what a  shame at least the voice acting is  absolutely excellent far better than  material deserved wither away from this  inaction  David Sebastian Cole should be damned  the credits roll after the button press  that serves as an ending and vital story  threads are left dangling in the wind  it's a rude send-off if you actually  wanted proper conclusion  the order is divided more or less  equally into fourth disparate pieces  cutscenes QTEs navigation and shooting  it strings these features into poorly  paced sequences that have no sense of  rhythm it is the modern action game  personified this is the part where I  walk for three minutes and now comes the  bit where I have to pick things up and  look at them for a couple of minutes and  then comes the brief shooting part that  practically ends before it begins  walking looking and shooting aren't bad  on their own of course what make such  basic mechanics so predictable in the  order is how they're used the downtime  is too often just walking without any  character development or creation of  tension the sequences in which you  investigate your surroundings for  important clues are even more tedious  you trudge about a room picking up  objects and turning them over in your  hand a few weapons aside you're rarely  looking at anything of interest old  photographs of characters you don't know  letters that provide the tiniest morsel  of backstory and so forth the order is  laughably in love with its own object  models you pick up minor knickknacks and  lovingly rotate them as if you have  discovered the Holy Grail I presume that  the order 1886 wishes to build its world  by demanding you admire these details  but the game didn't need to have tried  so hard  it's just absolutely attractive end of  story  the order clearly has cinematic  aspirations the camera is quite close to  your back to the point where you can't  see much once you've taken cover the  game is at its best when it allows  itself to be a shooter which is what  makes its failings all the more  disheartening the thermite rifle is one  of the most interesting weapons in  recent memory letting you fire a round  of magnesium ammo and then ignite it  with a rush of air what a blast it is to  mess with such a unique firearm and what  a heartbreaker to have such grossly  limited use of it the standard weapons  function just fine at least though this  is cover shooting at its most  predictable you walk into a room and see  all the obvious cover spots and sure  enough the enemies come in like  clockwork you shoot them all with ease  and move on to the next sequence there  is a mechanic that lets you slow down  time and Pelt enemies with bull  and another that lets you revive  yourself when you go down if you aren't  shot again both of these mechanics are  incredibly pointless though making it  easy to forget about them and indeed to  wonder why they're even there at all at  its best the order 1886 is perfectly  playable lovely to look at and listen to  a paragon of big-budget development but  it's also the face of mediocrity and  missed opportunities a bad game is one  thing but the order isn't bad it's just  boring everything to your liking thus  far quite just another ordinary London  morning  you
 

